Qt-wasm's shadow-DOM screen container never listens for browser focus
loss, so when the tab loses focus (user clicks into the URL bar, uses
a browser shortcut, switches tabs), the plugin still believes its
window is active. On return, QWasmWindow::pointerEventForWindow
short-circuits the call to requestActivate() because Qt thinks the
window is already focused, so the invisible m_focusHelper is never
re-focused and all subsequent keystrokes are lost until something
forces a full activation cycle (e.g. opening a menu popup).

Install a blur listener on window: when the browser tab actually loses
focus, clear Qt's focus window via handleFocusWindowChanged(nullptr).
The next PointerDown will then take the reactivation path and refocus
m_focusHelper as intended.

Listening on window.blur (rather than focusout on the shadow container)
is deliberate: focusout on the shadow host fires spuriously during
in-app focus transitions (menu popups opening, dialogs showing, etc.)
which would clear Qt's focus in the middle of a legitimate activation
sequence and leave popups marked "inactive".

--- a/src/plugins/platforms/wasm/qwasmscreen.h
+++ b/src/plugins/platforms/wasm/qwasmscreen.h
@@ -88,6 +88,7 @@ private:
     QWasmCursor m_cursor;
     static const char *m_canvasResizeObserverCallbackContextPropertyName;
     std::unique_ptr<qstdweb::EventCallback> m_onContextMenu;
+    std::unique_ptr<qstdweb::EventCallback> m_onWindowBlur;
 };

 QT_END_NAMESPACE
--- a/src/plugins/platforms/wasm/qwasmscreen.cpp
+++ b/src/plugins/platforms/wasm/qwasmscreen.cpp
@@ -71,6 +71,18 @@ QWasmScreen::QWasmScreen(const emscripten::val &containerOrCanvas)
     m_onContextMenu = std::make_unique<qstdweb::EventCallback>(
             m_shadowContainer, "contextmenu",
             [](emscripten::val event) { event.call<void>("preventDefault"); });
+    // Reset Qt's focus-window state when the browser tab loses focus
+    // (user clicks the URL bar, switches tabs, uses a browser shortcut).
+    // Without this, Qt still thinks its window is active on return and
+    // PointerDown short-circuits the reactivation path that focuses
+    // m_focusHelper, leaving the window keyboard-dead until a menu
+    // popup forces a real activation cycle.
+    m_onWindowBlur = std::make_unique<qstdweb::EventCallback>(
+            emscripten::val::global("window"), "blur",
+            [](emscripten::val event) {
+                Q_UNUSED(event);
+                QWindowSystemInterface::handleFocusWindowChanged(nullptr);
+            });
     // Create "specialHTMLTargets" mapping for the canvas - the element  might be unreachable based
     // on its id only under some conditions, like the target being embedded in a shadow DOM or a
     // subframe.
